Specification API
Description Demonstrates GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE operations

Background
Given base Page is : String
| https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com |

# The URL is built from three parts: the base Page above, the scenario's Page,
# and its Parameter. posts/1 is Page "posts" with Parameter "1".
#
# The status and the returned object are checked separately, because they come
# from different places -- the status is the HTTP status code and the body is
# the JSON the API sent back.
#
# jsonplaceholder returns the same data on every call, so every expected value
# is the actual one. Nothing here states a type in place of a value: a cell that
# said "string" would pass whatever the service returned, which is the one thing
# a test must not do.
#
# The body of post 1 is four lines, and a table cell holds one line, so it is
# written as the docstring Define PostOneBody at the foot of this file and
# referred to as =PostOneBody. The array scenario checks the number of posts
# rather than their contents, because one row cannot state a hundred distinct
# ones.

# ============================================================
# GET /posts/1
# ============================================================

Scenario Retrieve a single post
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | GET   |
| Page      | posts |
| Parameter | 1     |
| Body      |       |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 200 |
And response body is : Post
| userId | 1                                                                          |
| id     | 1                                                                          |
| title  | sunt aut facere repellat provident occaecati excepturi optio reprehenderit |
| body   | =PostOneBody                                                               |

# ============================================================
# GET /posts
# ============================================================

Scenario Retrieve all posts
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | GET   |
| Page      | posts |
| Parameter |       |
| Body      |       |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 200 |
# A hundred posts with a hundred distinct ids and titles: the count is what one
# row can state about them.
And response array contains this many items : Integer 
| 100 |

# ============================================================
# POST /posts
# ============================================================

Scenario Create a new post
Given new post data : NewPost
| title  | AlignThree Demo |
| body   | Testing POST    |
| userId | 7               |
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | POST    |
| Page      | posts   |
| Parameter |         |
| Body      | NewPost |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 201 |
# The service does not really store anything: it echoes the post back and always
# allocates 101, one past the 100 it serves.
And response body is : Post
| id     | 101             |
| title  | AlignThree Demo |
| body   | Testing POST    |
| userId | 7               |

# ============================================================
# PUT /posts/1
# ============================================================

Scenario Replace an existing post
Given replacement data : ReplacePost
| id     | 1              |
| userId | 1              |
| title  | Replaced Title |
| body   | Replaced Body  |
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | PUT         |
| Page      | posts       |
| Parameter | 1           |
| Body      | ReplacePost |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 200 |
And response body is : Post
| id     | 1              |
| userId | 1              |
| title  | Replaced Title |
| body   | Replaced Body  |

# ============================================================
# PATCH /posts/1
# ============================================================

Scenario Update a post title
Given patch data : PatchTitle
| title | Patched Title |
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | PATCH      |
| Page      | posts      |
| Parameter | 1          |
| Body      | PatchTitle |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 200 |
# PATCH replaces only the title; userId and body come back as post 1 already had
# them, so the body is the same four lines as the GET above.
And response body is : Post
| id     | 1             |
| userId | 1             |
| title  | Patched Title |
| body   | =PostOneBody  |

# ============================================================
# DELETE /posts/1
# ============================================================

# The response body is the empty JSON object {}, so there is nothing to check
# beyond the status.
Scenario Delete a post
When sending request : ApiRequest
| Method    | DELETE |
| Page      | posts  |
| Parameter | 1      |
| Body      |        |
Then response status is : ApiStatus
| Code | 200 |

Attributes ApiRequest
| Attribute | Type   | Default | Notes                                         |
| Method    | String |         | GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE                 |
| Page      | String |         | Appended to the base Page from the Background |
| Parameter | String |         | Appended after the Page, such as an id        |
| Body      | String |         | Name of the attribute set holding the body    |

Attributes ApiStatus
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes            |
| Code      | Integer | 200     | HTTP status code |

Attributes Post
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes |
| userId    | Integer |         |       |
| id        | Integer |         |       |
| title     | String  |         |       |
| body      | String  |         |       |

Attributes NewPost
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes |
| title     | String  |         |       |
| body      | String  |         |       |
| userId    | Integer |         |       |

Attributes ReplacePost
| Attribute | Type    | Default | Notes |
| id        | Integer |         |       |
| userId    | Integer |         |       |
| title     | String  |         |       |
| body      | String  |         |       |

Attributes PatchTitle
| Attribute | Type   | Default | Notes |
| title     | String |         |       |

# The body of post 1, exactly as the service returns it. Four lines, so it
# cannot be written in a cell; a docstring Define can hold it and =PostOneBody
# puts it in one.
Define PostOneBody
"""
quia et suscipit
suscipit recusandae consequuntur expedita et cum
reprehenderit molestiae ut ut quas totam
nostrum rerum est autem sunt rem eveniet architecto
"""
